<main>

<div class="print-page" style="--pc: #06b6d4">
  <header class="print-header">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumbs">
      <a href="/gcsl">GCSL</a>
    </nav>
    <h1>Development Tools</h1>
    <p class="print-note">Single-page view Â· <a href="/gcsl/tools/print.md">as markdown</a></p>
  </header>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="checking-and-formatting">Checking and Formatting Programs</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / Development Tools</p>
    </header>
    <p>You can check a GCSL program without running its effects, then format its
source consistently. The checker and formatter deliberately have different
scopes: checking needs workspace relationships; formatting only needs
source text that parses.</p>
<h2 id="checking-and-formatting--before-you-start">Before you start <a class="heading-anchor" href="#checking-and-formatting--before-you-start" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>You need <code>gcsl</code> and an existing source file or workspace. Commit or save
your changes before rewriting files so you can review the formatting diff.</p>
<h2 id="checking-and-formatting--check-and-format">Check and format <a class="heading-anchor" href="#checking-and-formatting--check-and-format" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p>Check a file, including its enclosing workspace if it has one:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="sh"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code><span class="hl-source hl-shell hl-bash"><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-shell"><span class="hl-variable hl-function hl-shell">gcsl</span></span><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-arguments hl-shell"> check main.gcsl</span>
</span></code></pre></div>
</li>
<li>
<p>Preview whether formatting would change that file:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="sh"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code><span class="hl-source hl-shell hl-bash"><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-shell"><span class="hl-variable hl-function hl-shell">gcsl</span></span><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-arguments hl-shell"> fmt<span class="hl-variable hl-parameter hl-option hl-shell"><span class="hl-punctuation hl-definition hl-parameter hl-shell"> --</span>check</span> main.gcsl</span>
</span></code></pre></div>
</li>
<li>
<p>Apply the formatter and repeat the check:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="sh"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code><span class="hl-source hl-shell hl-bash"><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-shell"><span class="hl-variable hl-function hl-shell">gcsl</span></span><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-arguments hl-shell"> fmt main.gcsl</span>
<span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-shell"><span class="hl-variable hl-function hl-shell">gcsl</span></span><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-arguments hl-shell"> fmt<span class="hl-variable hl-parameter hl-option hl-shell"><span class="hl-punctuation hl-definition hl-parameter hl-shell"> --</span>check</span> main.gcsl</span>
</span></code></pre></div>
</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="checking-and-formatting--check-the-result">Check the result <a class="heading-anchor" href="#checking-and-formatting--check-the-result" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>The checker reports its summary on standard error. Warnings alone do not
make it fail. The final formatting check succeeds without listing the
file when it is already formatted.</p>
<p>A file with lexical or syntax errors is left untouched by the formatter;
fix those diagnostics and retry. Formatting does not require a successful
type check. It uses one language style rather than editor-specific indent
settings.</p>
<h2 id="checking-and-formatting--checking-a-tree">Checking a tree <a class="heading-anchor" href="#checking-and-formatting--checking-a-tree" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>With no paths, both commands use the enclosing workspace and exclude
nested workspaces. An explicit directory makes them discover nested
workspaces too. However, <code>gcsl check DIRECTORY</code> checks workspaces, not
unattached <code>.gcsl</code> files under that directory. Pass each standalone file
explicitly when it also needs checking.</p>
<p>For automation, <code>gcsl check --message-format json main.gcsl</code> writes one
JSON diagnostic per line to standard output and keeps the summary on
standard error. See <code>gcsl(1)</code> for the schema and exit statuses.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="editor-integration">Configuring an Editor</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / Development Tools</p>
    </header>
    <p>The GCSL language server supplies diagnostics and navigation from the same
checker that runs programs. You can use it in an editor that supports the
Language Server Protocol (LSP) over standard input and standard output.</p>
<h2 id="editor-integration--before-you-start">Before you start <a class="heading-anchor" href="#editor-integration--before-you-start" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>You need the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>An editor with configurable LSP support.</li>
<li>The <code>gcsl</code> executable on the editor's command search path.</li>
<li>A GCSL file or workspace to open.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="editor-integration--connect-the-server">Connect the server <a class="heading-anchor" href="#editor-integration--connect-the-server" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<ol>
<li>Associate files ending in <code>.gcsl</code> with a GCSL language entry in your
editor.</li>
<li>Set the server command to <code>gcsl</code> and its argument list to <code>["lsp"]</code>.
Use standard input and standard output for transport.</li>
<li>Open a GCSL file. For a multifile project, open the workspace directory
so the editor can supply file changes and watch notifications.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="editor-integration--check-the-result">Check the result <a class="heading-anchor" href="#editor-integration--check-the-result" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Hover over a declared name to see its type. Introduce a misspelled name and
confirm that the editor displays a diagnostic. When an unambiguous
spelling suggestion is available, a quick fix offers the replacement.</p>
<p>The server checks source but does not evaluate programs. It supports
completion, signature help, inlay hints, formatting, symbol search, and
references. Rename checks the proposed edits before returning them and
refuses changes that introduce errors or change name resolution. It does
not rename files, directories, or standard-library declarations.</p>
<h2 id="editor-integration--incomplete-source">Incomplete source <a class="heading-anchor" href="#editor-integration--incomplete-source" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>The index retains useful information around many syntax errors. For an
unbalanced block, a query can use a repaired copy to answer navigation or
completion while diagnostics still describe the real text. Rename needs
the real source to be valid enough to verify and can be refused instead.</p>
<p>Editor syntax highlighting is a separate integration. The grammar and
queries in <code>tree-sitter-gcsl</code> are maintained in their own repository; they
are not required for the <code>gcsl lsp</code> process to check your code.</p>

  </section>
</div>

</main>

